; Composite-sync + RGB test-pattern generator for the FreeWili OG breakout
; header. Two state machines drive two physically disjoint pin groups --
; GPIO 8-15 (8 bits: B2 B1 B0 G2 G1 G0 R2 R1) and GPIO 26-27 (2 bits: R0,
; CSYNC) -- because those are the only ten breakout-header pins the
; direction-control system (bsp/main_cpu/gpio/breakout.c) actually covers;
; GPIO 16/17 are excluded (see apps/tpg's design notes: they are the I2C0
; breakout pair and may not be plain push-pull capable).
;
; Both programs are the same shape -- pull one 32-bit word, apply it to the
; pins, then hold for N more cycles -- so as long as neither DMA feed ever
; lets its FIFO run dry, the two buses stay cycle-locked against each other
; for the entire run (PIO execution is fully deterministic; nothing but a
; FIFO stall can introduce drift between two SMs given identical per-word
; cycle counts).
;
; Word format, video_pattern.c's packing:
;   bits [0 : N-1]  = pin pattern (N = 8 for bus8, 2 for bus2)
;   bits [N : 31]   = hold_cycles - 3
;
; The "-3" accounts for the two `out` instructions (1 cycle each) plus the
; final loop iteration, which executes but does not branch when X reaches
; zero: loading X=(hold-3) makes the `jmp x--` loop run exactly hold-2
; times (X counts hold-3, hold-4, ..., 0 -- that is hold-2 distinct
; iterations), and 2 (outs) + (hold-2) (loop) = hold cycles total, matching
; the caller's intended hold_cycles exactly. video_pattern.c must never be
; asked for a hold shorter than 3 cycles -- the tightest real segment here
; is the 460-cycle equalizing pulse, far above that floor.

.program tpg_bus8
.wrap_target
    out pins, 8
    out x, 24
delay8:
    jmp x--, delay8
.wrap

.program tpg_bus2
.wrap_target
    out pins, 2
    out x, 30
delay2:
    jmp x--, delay2
.wrap
